to see  any child die is heartbreaking  but to see children dying  i think it was a rhetor and carrier  from starvation and from being frozen to  death  not only breaks your heart but makes you  angry that this is avoidable  and that these are unnecessary deaths  and that we have a duty of care of  course we have to blame the taliban  but when you see such a level of  suffering  you've got to act  and of course the way we must do this  now  is that all the western nations that  were part of the afghanistan effort must  come together and they must provide the  four billion of humanitarian aid that is  being asked for so that we can have  proper health care  so we can feed children and families and  so girls can go back to school and it's  now urgent in the next few days  that the british government take a lead  call a pledging conference bring these  countries together get the money out  there and it is possible to avoid what  is a winter of starvation  and the loss of hundreds of thousands of  lives unnecessarily but how to deal with  the political situation how to deal with  the taliban if the resources are sent  into afghanistan at the moment the  danger is that the  taliban appropriated it doesn't go to  the people that need it that's why  sanctions are in place  no you're wrong uh  the the aid can get through through ngos  through the united nations organizations  i'm in direct touch with the un  humanitarian coordinator he can get aid  in through unicef he can get aid in  through a number of organizations like  save the children at the international  rescue committee the red cross all these  organizations can operate within  afghanistan without giving credibility  or legitimacy to the taliban and i think  it's just a myth that is being  perpetuated across here that the only  way to get aid to people in afghanistan  is to get it through the taliban now of  course there are issues that have got to  be dealt with and i'm very happy to  address them but the important point is  that when you see suffering you can do  something about it and the ngos and the  un organizations are there and ready to  act but what they don't have is the  money to do so  and very few countries have given enough  money  to meet what has been a humanitarian  appeal that came before christmas uh  from the united nations for 4.4 billion  dollars  to allow them to feed 23 million people  and the longer we wait in giving the  money that's necessary and this can only  be done by calling countries together in  some sort of pledging conference the  longer we wait the more people will die  and the more this will encourage  terrorists the more this will encourage  the heroin trade because that's the only  occupation where people are making money  in afghanistan and the more we will see  mass migration out of afghanistan with  all the problems that that entails so  it's not only a moral duty on our part  it's enlightened self-interest for us to  act it's interesting what you say mr  brown there about then the the  infrastructure is in place through the  ngos it's it's more about the lack of  resources the lack of money being  pledged then what though are the  obstacles within afghanistan you did  allude to let's explore those a little  bit further  well let's first of all be clear that  countries could give money now  and that's the biggest issue that your  audience will be wanting to think about  how can we help stop  uh nadia orezza these girls starving to  death or being frozen to death how can  we give the medical treatment to  girls in hospital as the babies are born  and we saw the difficult birth without  the medical equipment that really was  necessary to make sure that our life was  going to be safe now this is our  responsibility we can talk all night  about what uh we think about the taliban  we can talk all night about what we  should do about the sanctions regime  i've got many ideas about that but the  important thing surely is this uh that  your reporter alex crawford has bravely  brought these photographs to us these  pictures of such suffering and there is  a duty on our part to do something about  it  but but you you did say you would  explore further any of the obstacles in  afghanistan because you know when we're  talking about little girls like that  there is a question isn't there of  course there is about what happens  hopefully they and hundreds of thousands  of others survive what happens when they  want to go to the school of their choice  any school indeed what happens when they  want to to marry the person that they  want to what happens if they then decide  that they want to not wear the dress  that the taliban want them to wear these  are important questions as well mr brown  yes and of course and there are extra  judicial killings and nobody can support  the oppressive repressive  policies toward women and girls of the  taliban regime and you'll notice that i  said the taliban is to blame right at  the beginning for what is going on here  but the the important thing to recognize  is that we can put pressure to get girls  back to school i'm actually chairman of  education cannot wait which is the  organization that's helping  girls particularly who are displaced and  who are refugees and with unicef working  in afghanistan education cannot wait  working many ngos working the taliban  have now set a date for teenage girls to  be able to go back to school on march  the 23rd  and we must ensure that the money is  there for these schools to be open  so that these girls can go back now we  can lay down conditions and say no money  will come unless the girls can actually  go to school  but what would be unfair i think to the  girls into their future is to say that  we are going to do nothing having spent  20 years in afghanistan saying we're  there to help the afghan people i think  it's our duty to continue to offer help  subject to the conditions that i'm  laying down okay and can you see a time  coming when the taliban can be should be  recognized as the  legitimate government of afghanistan  well i think there are discussions  taking place in norway at the moment as  you know and  the question of what kind of  representative government afghanistan is  going to have in the future is still  still an issue but really for me  watching these pictures uh tonight that  is not the main immediate issue  and while people may want to make this a  political issue  this is actually a humanitarian issue  it's about saving lives that are about  to be lost  it's about a winter when every day more  and more people are going to die and i  repeat the only way to get money that  will help  provide food and provide health care and  get the schools open is for the  international community to come together  it is quite shocking that after so many  months almost six months since the  taliban took over and we've seen this  humanitarian crisis and warned about it  there has been no pledging conference  organized by any single country prime  minister or president when the need is  so obvious  and it's of a humanitarian issue that  has got to be resolved so the first  thing that i want to see happen is the  prime minister of britain talks to the  european union and to joe biden in  america they arrange a pledging  conference get the money in show that we  can help people and save lives and i do  think that would stand us in good stead  for any negotiations we've got to have  in the future we do not need to  recognize the legitimacy of the taliban  regime to get aid to people who are  suffering do you think that that uh lack  of planning for the humanitarian  situation is a function of the the  overall chaotic withdrawal there wasn't  much planning for that either was there  well i think we've learned a lot from  what happened in august but again you  know we've got to look forward this is a  matter of hours and days not a matter of  months and years  and we can sit speculating in london  about what's uh the right or the wrong  uh attitude that was taken by joe biden  in august what we've got to do is face  up to the humanitarian crisis that  resulted and the longer the west runs  away from the actions uh that uh have  now  befallen the what's befallen the afghan  people the more we will suffer in the  long run because these extremists will  argue  that  the west did not come to help people who  when they were in need and that  coexistence is impossible and we can  stop that argument and stop giving that  argument to terrorists by getting  humanitarian aid we did it in zimbabwe  when we disagreed with the regime we're  doing it in other countries where there  are people in peril i think we've got to  admit that this is the biggest  humanitarian crisis of our times  and if we do nothing  we're talking about the lives of  hundreds of thousands of people and  perhaps millions are put at risk through  starvation and mr brown can you give us  any hope on that front is there any  cause for even a slight degree of  optimism that we may get this pledging  conference or certainly a virtual one as  soon as we possibly can  well i've heard to the foreign secretary  weeks ago and i'm waiting for a reply  i i i do think that britain has a  particular look as he here we want to  show that we are a global power  and i think  we can work with the european union and  co-convene such a conference uh i i  think america probably is the it will  join it but i don't think they'll be the  leaders of it the organization of  islamic states i think will want to be  part of it because they've already  pledged that they want to do something  britain gave just over 90 million last  week but that is not enough we're  talking about 4 billion and more that is  actually needed and martin griffis who's  the u.n coordinator a person who was a  british civil servant at one point i  mean he knows just how big that need is  and how it's got to be met urgently and  it's about time 